# Linux Clone Post-Setup Checklist

After cloning a Linux machine, several system identifiers should be regenerated to avoid conflicts with other cloned VMs on the network, DHCP, monitoring systems, clusters, or SSH.

## Important Items to Change

* Hostname
* Machine ID
* SSH host keys
* Network interface MAC address
* Static IP address (if configured)
* Service UUIDs (depending on the environment)

---

## Change Hostname

```bash
sudo hostnamectl set-hostname NEW-HOSTNAME
```

---

## Regenerate Machine ID

Important for systemd, D-Bus, and some management tools.

```bash
sudo truncate -s 0 /etc/machine-id
sudo rm -f /var/lib/dbus/machine-id
sudo systemd-machine-id-setup
```

---

## Regenerate SSH Host Keys

Otherwise all cloned machines will share the same SSH fingerprint.

```bash
sudo rm -f /etc/ssh/ssh_host_*
sudo dpkg-reconfigure openssh-server
```

---

## Change MAC Address

If you are using VMware, Proxmox, VirtualBox, or another hypervisor, assign a new MAC address from the VM settings.

This is important to avoid DHCP and ARP conflicts.

---

## Update Static Network Configuration

If the system uses a static IP address, update the network configuration accordingly.

Example for Ubuntu Netplan:

```bash
sudo nano /etc/netplan/*.yaml
sudo netplan apply
```

---

## Remove Old DHCP Leases (Optional)

```bash
sudo rm -f /var/lib/dhcp/*
```

---

## Cloud-Init Cleanup

If cloud-init is installed, it may restore previous hostname or network settings.

```bash
sudo cloud-init clean
```

---

## Recommended Template Preparation

Before creating a VM template:

* Clear machine-id
* Remove SSH host keys
* Install and configure cloud-init

This allows every cloned VM to generate unique identifiers automatically during first boot.
